# card.zd — the three presentational widgets, and what each is for.
#
# Nothing here holds state and nothing here handles a key. They exist
# because the *markup* is the decision: which element carries the box,
# whether the box has a heading, and what a reader who cannot see it is
# told. Every program gets those three wrong in a different way, and they
# are wrong in ways no stylesheet can repair.
#
# NO COLOURS, NO SIZES, NO SPACING — here or anywhere in this directory.
# §6.1's answer to appearance is `class is "…"`, so every widget takes a
# `class` and passes it to the element that carries the box. A widget set
# that shipped a look would compete with Tailwind instead of composing
# with it, and a caller who wanted a different look would have to fight
# the default rather than write one.

# A titled box, and the title is the point.
#
# `Section` rather than `Column`, and that is the whole argument for this
# component existing. §16.3.6 makes `Section` a *sectioning* element, so
# the `Heading` inside it is one level deeper than the heading outside it,
# chosen by nesting rather than written down. A card built from a `Column`
# and a `Heading` produces a second `h1` on the page, which is the outline
# failure a program cannot see and a screen reader user navigates by.
#
# A card with no heading is not this. It is `Column class is "…"`, which
# needs no widget, and giving one an empty title would put an empty
# heading in the outline — worse than no card at all.
component Card with title, class, children
    Section class is class
        Heading title
        children

# A short label beside something else: a count, a state, a tag.
#
# One element deep, deliberately. What a badge gets wrong is never its
# markup, it is that the meaning lives in the colour — a red pill and a
# green pill, identical to a screen reader and to a reader with protanopia.
# `label` is required and positional, so the meaning is in the text, and
# the colour is a `class` the caller writes on top of text that already
# says it.
component Badge with label, class
    Text label, class is class

# Nothing here yet, and what to do about it.
#
# The three parts are what an empty state is: what is missing, why, and
# the way out. `children` is the way out, so the caller nests whatever the
# action is — a `Button`, a `Link`, a `Form` — rather than this component
# taking a label and a handler it would have to invent a shape for.
#
# `Column` and not `Section`: an empty state stands in for content that
# would have been inside whatever contains it, so it is not a subordinate
# section of that thing. Its `Heading` is therefore at the depth of the
# content it replaces, which is where a reader expects to find it.
component EmptyState with title, detail, class, children
    Column class is class
        Heading title
        Paragraph detail
        children
